+### Instanced Drawing and Batch Merging
+Merges terrain tiles with the same LOD level into a single draw call using instanced drawing to reduce CPU overhead from issuing many draw calls [S3]. Each instance passes the tile’s world transformation matrix and texture atlas offset as instance data [S3]. The number of draw calls is reduced from $M$ (total visible tiles) to the number of distinct LOD levels (typically no more than 10), where $M$ has LOD level distribution $c_0, c_1, \dots, c_{max}$ [S3].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ for instance data upload and $O(1)$ for draw call overhead, where $N$ is the number of instances [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for instance buffers. For the explicit attribute layout, this is $10 \times N \times 4$ bytes (3 pos + 1 scale + 3 color + 3 padding/alignment) [S1, unofficial]. For the matrix layout, it is $21 \times N \times 4$ bytes [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for instance buffers, consuming $10 \times N \times 4$ bytes for the explicit layout or $21 \times N \times 4$ bytes for the matrix layout [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
 *   **Measured Performance:** Renders 250,000 cubes (500x500 grid) in a single draw call [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Bypasses CPU bottlenecks by reducing draw calls to one [S1, unofficial]. Simple to implement with standard WebGL 2 features.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires CPU-side update of instance data every frame if dynamic [S2, unofficial]. Precision issues may arise with large grid offsets [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Bypasses CPU bottlenecks by reducing draw calls to one [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires CPU-side updates of instance data every frame if dynamic [S2, unofficial].
 *   **When to Use:** Large static or semi-static voxel grids where CPU draw call overhead is prohibitive [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Highly dynamic scenes with frequent instance additions/removals where indirect draw calls might be more efficient [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-This technique is widely used in voxel-based games and procedural terrain rendering. It was popularized by projects like *CubeWorld* which demonstrated rendering large voxel worlds using hardware instancing [S1, unofficial]. The specific implementation details (21 floats per cube, health values) are documented in *CubeEngine* [S2, unofficial].
